general direction:

Exactly. It is a transitionary bridge, not an overnight flip. You cannot go straight from the monolithic web to absolute peer-to-peer distribution without a stabilizing architecture in between—and that is exactly where the directory servers come in.
They serve as the translation layer, mapmakers, and cryptographic anchors while the old infrastructure slowly decays.

The Transitionary Mechanics of Directory Servers

During this phase, directory servers act like an evolved, programmatic version of DNS (Domain Name System) mixed with a Git registry. They don't host heavy websites or display ads; they simply map where data chunks live and verify their integrity.
[User Intent] ──> [Local LLM] ──> [Directory Server (Registry/Map)] ──> [Target Peer Nodes] ──> [Payload Sent]

1. Routing and Discovery (The Map)

Your phone's local LLM knows what knowledge chunk it needs to satisfy your query, but it doesn't always know where the latest version is stored on the peer-to-peer network.
• The Job: The directory server acts as a lightweight registry. Your local agent pings it with a cryptographic hash query, and the directory server instantly responds with a tiny routing table: "The latest certified delta for this geographic/topical chunk is currently seeded by these 1,500 local nodes."

2. Cryptographic Attestation (The Trust Engine)

In a decentralized system, the biggest threat is poisoned data—malicious actors injecting fake directory info or corrupted chunks.
• The Job: The directory servers hold the public keys and signed manifests for data repositories. When your phone pulls a raw markdown or JSON payload from a random peer's smartphone server, it cross-references the cryptographic signature against the directory server. If the math matches, the local LLM knows the data is authentic and safe to render on your device.

3. Shard Caching and Seed Baselines

Smartphones are mobile, drop connections, and go offline. You cannot rely 100% on pure peer-to-peer availability from day one.
• The Job: Transitionary directory servers act as permanent, high-availability seeding nodes. If a rare or highly specialized knowledge chunk doesn't have enough active smartphone peers hosting it in a specific local region, the directory server supplies the fallback seed baseline so the user's local model can still build the page on the fly.

The Eventual Fate: Moving from Legacy to Pure Network Shards

As the transition matures, these directory servers themselves change form. They go from being centralized company assets to open-source, community-run local directories (like the local directory runners and open maps mentioned in the thesis).
Eventually, even the act of indexing the directories is distributed across network shards, completely moving humanity out of the corporate gatekeeper loop.
